* ============================================================================
* m16_guard_regression.dts
*
* PACK AND MEMO GC MUST REFUSE A MEMO WIDTH THEY CANNOT DECODE.
*
* EXPECTED MARKER COUNT: 8  (M16_G1..G3, M16_T1..T5)
*
* FIRST RUN 2026-10-20: 5 of 5 markers printed, ONE GREEN AND FOUR RED, and
*   NONE of the four reds was the guard failing. Three were one bug in this
*   file -- SET PATH is DATA-relative, so "SET PATH DBF sandbox" pointed at
*   data\sandbox, a directory that EXISTS, so there was no hard error: the
*   catalog copy was never found and the fixture was created in the wrong
*   place. The fourth was a REAL ENGINE DEFECT in PACK's memo path, recorded
*   at the contrast arm below. The arm earned its place on its first run.
*
* THIRD RUN 2026-10-20: 8 of 8 GREEN, including the M(8) memo arm that the fix
*   repaired -- "Sidecar(s): DBF and DTX rebuilt and synchronized." But the
*   wrapper still said FAIL, and its reason was ITS OWN over-broad check, not
*   this spec: it swept the whole sandbox for *.pack.tmp.* and found
*   PKSRC.pack.tmp.dbf dated SEP 14, a month-old artifact of the primary-key
*   lane. Scoped to this spec's own three tables. An assertion too WIDE fails
*   for other people's state; every other instrument this session was too
*   NARROW. Both are the same mistake about what the question is.
*
* AND ERASE WAS A DRY RUN IN BOTH RUNS. "ERASE <table>" without CONFIRM prints
*   what it would delete and does nothing -- cmd_erase.cpp's documented safety
*   gate. The cleanup this spec intended never happened; CREATE overwrote the
*   table anyway, so nothing failed, which is exactly why it went unnoticed.
*   TWICE NOW I HAVE GUESSED A COMMAND'S SPELLING INSTEAD OF READING THE
*   WORKING SPEC IN THE SAME DIRECTORY: SET PATH DBF sandbox (should have been
*   DBF/SANDBOX) and ERASE without CONFIRM. copy_table_regression.dts has had
*   both right since it was written.
*   THAT NUMBER IS THE PRIMARY INSTRUMENT. An errored marker PRINTS NOTHING
*   rather than going red, so a lost claim leaves this suite in silence while
*   every remaining arm still reads green. Count them in the transcript. Four
*   green is a FAILURE that looks like a pass, and which one vanished is the
*   first thing to find out.
*
* WHY THIS SPEC EXISTS, AND WHY IT WAS CLAIMED IMPOSSIBLE TWICE
*   cmd_create.cpp hard-codes memo width -- 8 for x64, 10 for legacy -- and
*   DISCARDS the width the caller asked for, so no CREATE can build an M(16)
*   table. The only producer in the tree is cmd_workspace.cpp. From that I
*   twice concluded no .dts could test this, and handed over a fourteen-line
*   hand procedure instead. IT WAS NOT RUN, TWICE, OVER FIVE DAYS.
*
*   THE ERROR WAS CONFLATING TWO THINGS: "no script can CREATE an M(16) table"
*   is true; "no script can TEST one" is false. The fixture is a FILE COPY of
*   the workspace catalog, made by tmp/verify_m16_guard.ps1 before this runs.
*   This spec only OPENS it.
*
*   AND THAT IS ALSO WHY NO CatalogBracket IS NEEDED. The bracket exists for
*   specs that MINT catalog rows through WORKSPACE NEW/SAVE. This spec MINTS NO
*   CATALOG ROW -- its only WORKSPACE verb is CLOSE ALL, which closes areas
*   and writes nothing -- and it writes no file outside DBF\SANDBOX. The
*   production catalog is never opened, so the 2.3 MB stranding event this
*   guard prevents cannot be caused by the test for it.
*
* FIXTURE REQUIRED, and this spec CANNOT build it:
*   dottalkpp\data\dbf\sandbox\PK16CAT.dbf  + .dtx
*   Run tmp\verify_m16_guard.ps1 first; it copies the live catalog and then
*   invokes this file. If M16_G1 reds, the fixture is missing or is not the
*   catalog -- every arm below is then meaningless, which is what G1 is for.
*
* WHY RECORD 4 IS THE DISCRIMINATOR, measured 2026-10-20 against the copy:
*   rec 4 is NOT delete-flagged in either state, so no arm has to read a
*   deleted record, and the name differs between the two outcomes:
*
*     unpacked (PACK REFUSED)   rec 4 WS_NAME = mcc_x64
*     packed   (PACK RAN)       rec 4 WS_NAME = sess_cursor
*
*   293 rows, 148 delete-flagged, so a successful pack leaves 145 and shifts
*   everything from rec 1 onward. Record 1 would also discriminate and is
*   REJECTED on purpose: it is delete-flagged, and an arm that depends on
*   reading a deleted record depends on SET DELETED, which is not this spec's
*   subject.
*
* WHAT NO MARKER HERE CAN ASSERT, stated rather than implied:
*   THE REFUSAL TEXT. No marker in this language reads console output, so
*   "NOT SCANNED -- 1 memo field(s) ... (SNAPSHOT)" and "PACK: refused --
*   ... cannot decode: SNAPSHOT" are run for the TRANSCRIPT and proved only by
*   EFFECT. Read them with your eyes; the arms prove the file did not change.
*   THE SIDECAR BYTES. A marker cannot stat a file. tmp\verify_m16_guard.ps1
*   compares PK16CAT.dbf and .dtx before and after and is the other half of
*   this proof. Markers for data in the engine; PowerShell for bytes on disk.
*
* THE CONTRAST ARM IS NOT OPTIONAL. A BROKEN PACK REFUSES EVERYTHING, so
* M16_T1 alone is worthless. M16_T2 and M16_T3 prove PACK still compacts in
* this process on this build. They use a PLAIN x64 table; the reason, and the
* defect that forced it, are at the arm itself.
*
* GUARDS EXIST BECAUSE BOTH ARMS READ A VALUE THAT A DEAD FIXTURE ALSO
* PRODUCES. M16_G1 proves the catalog copy is open and rec 4 reads what this
* spec was written against. M16_G2 proves the M(8) fixture got three rows
* before anything is deleted or packed -- without it, a CREATE that silently
* wrote nothing would leave M16_T2 reading a blank and going red for a reason
* that has nothing to do with PACK. That is the G6/G7 lesson from the primary
* key spec, designed in rather than discovered after.
*
* MUTATION PASS OWED on all five arms. None has been shown able to red.
* Until then they are decoration. The honest way to earn M16_T1 is to stash
* the guard in cmd_pack.cpp, re-run, and watch it go red on a real pack.
* ============================================================================

STOP_ON_ERROR OFF
? "M16-GUARD-PROOF-BEGIN"

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
* SET PATH IS DATA-RELATIVE, NOT DBF-RELATIVE, and the first run of this spec
* proved it the expensive way: "SET PATH DBF sandbox" resolved to
* data\sandbox -- a directory that EXISTS, so there was no hard error. The
* catalog copy was looked for there and not found, and CREATE then wrote the
* M(8) fixture there too. Four arms went red for a reason unrelated to what
* they test. copy_table_regression.dts has had it right all along:
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX

* ---- the M(16) fixture: a copy of the live catalog, opened, never minted ----
SELECT 1
USE PK16CAT
GO 4
? "M16_G1_fixture_is_the_catalog_copy:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "mcc_x64")

* ---- the two refusals. For the transcript; proved by effect below. ----
MEMO STATUS
MEMO GC CONFIRM
PACK

* ---- BOTH BRANCHES MUST LAND READABLE. A refused PACK leaves the table OPEN;
* ---- a successful one leaves it CLOSED. CLOSE then USE handles either, and a
* ---- plain second USE of a still-open table would print "already open" and
* ---- NOT change the current area, which would read the wrong thing silently.
CLOSE
SELECT 1
USE PK16CAT
GO 4
? "M16_T1_pack_refused_on_m16_catalog:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "mcc_x64")
CLOSE

* ---- THE CONTRAST ARM: PACK must still compact a PLAIN x64 table ----
* IT WAS AN M(8) MEMO TABLE ON THE FIRST RUN AND THAT RUN FOUND A REAL DEFECT,
* which is why it is plain now. On 2026-10-20, build Oct 15 09:32:22, the M(8)
* arm produced:
*
*   PACK: Failed to rename original DTX to backup: The process cannot access
*         the file because it is being used by another process.
*   PACK: failed replacing original DBF/DTX with packed pair
*   PACK: operation aborted. Table remains closed.
*
* MEASURED CAUSE: cmd_pack.cpp calls A.close() but NEVER calls
* cli_memo::memo_auto_on_close(A) -- the string appears zero times in that file
* -- so the AREA's MemoStore, placed in g_store by memo_auto_on_use when the
* table was opened, still holds the .dtx open across the whole pack.
* pack_x64_memo_table closes its own oldStore and newStore before renaming, so
* the leaked handle is the area's. PACK REQUIRES AN OPEN TABLE, so for an x64
* memo table that handle is ALWAYS present and the rename ALWAYS fails.
*
* So PACK's advertised M(8) memo support -- its own usage block says "x64 M(8)
* memo tables are packed by rebuilding BOTH the DBF and the DTX sidecar" --
* appears never to have worked on a table with deleted rows. The two earlier
* runtime measurements both missed it: one had zero deleted rows, the other was
* the catalog, which takes the plain path.
*
* THE FIX IS ONE LINE AND IT IS A SEPARATE COMMIT, by owner ruling of
* 2026-10-20 ("two commits, guard first"). A permanently red arm must not enter
* this suite -- that is DEF_FAMILY's mistake, markers with no grader -- so this
* arm uses a PLAIN x64 table today and the M(8) memo arm arrives with its fix.
*
* WHAT THE PLAIN ARM STILL PROVES, and it is the whole job of a contrast arm:
* PACK compacts in this process on this build. A broken PACK refuses
* everything, so without this M16_T1 is worthless. What it no longer proves is
* that the MEMO-AWARE path works -- stated here rather than implied, because
* that is exactly the claim the deferred commit has to earn.
SELECT 2
ERASE PKPLAIN CONFIRM
CREATE X64 PKPLAIN (ID N(4), MARK C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE MARK WITH "A"
APPEND
REPLACE MARK WITH "B"
APPEND
REPLACE MARK WITH "C"
GO 3
? "M16_G2_plain_fixture_has_three_rows:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "C")

GO 1
DELETE
PACK

SELECT 2
USE PKPLAIN
GO 2
? "M16_T2_pack_still_compacts_a_plain_x64_table:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "C")
GO 1
? "M16_T3_plain_pack_dropped_the_deleted_row:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "B")
CLOSE

* ---- THE M(8) MEMO ARM: the path the one-line fix repaired ----
* THIS ARM IS WHY THE FIX IS NOT A CLAIM. It was removed from this spec on
* 2026-10-20 because it was RED for a real reason -- cmd_pack.cpp released the
* DBF handle and never the sidecar, so the DTX rename always failed -- and a
* permanently red arm must not sit in a suite. It returns with the fix that
* makes it green, which is the order the house asks for: red for a reason, then
* the fix, then green.
*
* IT IS NOT A DUPLICATE OF THE PLAIN ARM. 'BODY M' becomes M(8) on an x64
* table, so this exercises pack_x64_memo_table -- the memo-aware path that
* rebuilds BOTH files and remaps object ids. The plain arm above never enters
* that function. Both are kept: the plain one proves PACK compacts at all, this
* one proves the memo path does.
*
* WHAT THIS ARM STILL CANNOT SEE, and the wrapper checks instead: that the
* rebuilt sidecar's remapped ids, CRCs and surviving payloads are correct. A
* marker reads a field, not a sidecar. Record 2 reading C proves the DBF was
* compacted; it does not prove the DTX is sound. That measurement is still
* nobody's, and it is named here so it is not assumed.
SELECT 3
ERASE PK8M CONFIRM
CREATE X64 PK8M (ID N(4), MARK C(10), BODY M)
APPEND
REPLACE MARK WITH "A"
APPEND
REPLACE MARK WITH "B"
APPEND
REPLACE MARK WITH "C"
GO 3
? "M16_G3_m8_memo_fixture_has_three_rows:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "C")

GO 1
DELETE
PACK

SELECT 3
USE PK8M
GO 2
? "M16_T4_pack_compacts_an_m8_memo_table:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "C")
GO 1
? "M16_T5_m8_memo_pack_dropped_the_deleted_row:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "B")
CLOSE

? "M16-GUARD-PROOF-END"
